  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "As Dmitry Nagiyev protests that his bride cannot fire everyone he smiles at, he compliments the female pianist's fingers—prompting Eleonora to fire her too and appoint waiter Nikita as the new restaurant director.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Нельзя так делать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Нельзя увольнять всех, кому я улыбаюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Какие красивые у вас пальцы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "А что вы делаете сегодня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы уволены.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И ваши пальцы тоже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну ладно, ты уволила всех.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "А кто работать будет сегодня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никита, иди сюда!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поздравляю, ты директор ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Спасибо, Элеонора Андреевна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Никита Андреевич, кофе?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Нельзя так делать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot act like this.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'one must not' and finishing on 'to do'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Нельзя увольнять всех, кому я улыбаюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot fire everyone I smile at.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'smile', turning the action back on the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Какие красивые у вас пальцы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What beautiful fingers you have.' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'fingers' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "А что вы делаете сегодня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what are you doing today?' Dmitry Nagiyev chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'tonight' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы уволены.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are fired.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'you' and finishing on 'to fire'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И ваши пальцы тоже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And your fingers too.' Eleonora anchors the line on 'and', while 'also' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну ладно, ты уволила всех.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright then, you fired everyone.' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'everyone' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "А кто работать будет сегодня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then who is going to run things today?' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'tonight' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Eleonora answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никита, иди сюда!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nikita, come here!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Nikita' before landing on 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поздравляю, ты директор ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Congratulations, you are the restaurant director.' The meaning hinges on 'congratulations' working together with 'restaurant' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Спасибо, Элеонора Андреевна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, Eleonora Andreyevna.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'thank you' before landing on 'Eleonora'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Никита Андреевич, кофе?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nikita Andreyevich, coffee?' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'Nikita' and 'coffee' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
